          "session_id": "full-g-md-029",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "Two courses of steroid tablets in a year is not something these sources treat as proof of poor control. The guidance I could find says that poor control is usually a treatment, adherence or inhaler-technique problem rather than proof of severe disease (*Asthma*, General clinical reference). Whether your course count matters for you is a question for your clinician. If your breathing is getting worse quickly, or you struggle to speak or catch your breath, seek urgent or emergency help now and follow your action plan.\n\nA technique review is a check at an appointment, where a clinician or nurse watches you use your device, because that shows more than a leaflet does. Common mistakes depend on the device. Pressurised inhalers often fail when the press and the breath are not coordinated. Dry powder inhalers need a fast, deep breath in, and they fail if you breathe out into them. If you use two types, you may be asked to do two different things, so each one needs checking. Rinsing your mouth after a steroid inhaler can reduce hoarseness and thrush (*Sticking with asthma treatment*, General clinical reference).\n\nOn stepping up, the asthma guidance describes a stepwise approach, and it says each step is reviewed rather than fixed. Before stepping up, it says inhaler technique and adherence should be checked, because they explain a large share of apparent treatment failure. It warns that a step-up which compensates for poor technique treats the wrong problem (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach*). I could not retrieve the specific criteria for when a step-up is appropriate, so I can't say where you would fall. For COPD, the retrieved passage says a significant exacerbation history leads to dual long-acting bronchodilator therapy, with an inhaled steroid added where blood eosinophil counts are high or asthma features coexist. That passage was cut off, so the full sequence is not available to me. I also could not find evidence on how strong these recommendations are, or any place where the authorities disagree.\n\nYour question doesn't say whether you have asthma or COPD, and I can't tell from here. Bring these to your next review: Has my technique been checked, and can you watch me use it? Do I use more than one device, and is my technique right for each? Am I taking my controller daily, or only when symptomatic, and which inhaler is my reliever? Does my written action plan match what I actually do? What does my control look like now, in daytime symptoms, night waking and reliever use? Before any change, what checks will you make first?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
